===Etymology 2===
From {{compound|en|it|-'s|alt2=-’s|pos2=possessive marker}}.

====Determiner====
{{head|en|determiner|cat2=possessive determiners}}

# {{obsolete form of|en|its}}.
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=43|book=I|passage=The manner wherewith our Lawes assay to moderate the foolish and vaine expences of table-cheare and apparell, seemeth contrarie to '''it’s''' end.}}
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|book=I|chapter=Introduction|section=section II (Of the Nature of Laws in General)|pages=42–43|pageref=42|passage=Thoſe human laws, that annex a puniſhment to it, do not at all increaſe '''it’s''' moral guilt, or ſuperadd any freſh obligation ''[[in foro conscientiae|in foro conſcientiae]]'' to abſtain from '''it’s''' perpetration.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1767|author=George Canning|chapter=Anti-Lucretius|title=Poems|location=London|publisher=for the author|page=281|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/007697226
|passage=Behold the balanc’d Scales suspended stand,<br>Neither a jot inclin’d to either hand:<br>But place the smallest grain of weight in one,<br>Straight the fraternal equipoise is gone;<br>The loaded scale, preponderant, downward flies,<br>Drags down the chain, and makes '''it’s''' partner rise.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1787|title=w:United States Constitution|chapter={{w|Article One of the United States Constitution|Article I}}
|passage=No State shall, without the Consent of the Congress, lay any Imposts or Duties on Imports or Exports, except what may be absolutely necessary for executing '''it's''' inspection Laws[.]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1803|author=President Thomas Jefferson|title=Instructions to Captain Meriwether Lewis
|passage=The object of your mission is to explore the Missouri river, & such principal stream of it, as, by '''it’s''' course & communication with the waters of the Pacific Ocean.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Emma|volume=III|chapter=IV|page=60|passage='''It’s''' tendency would be to raise and refine her mind—and it must be saving her from the danger of degradation.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1848
|title=w:Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo
|url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Guadalupe_Hidalgo_(original)
|text=Article V.<br>The Boundary line between the two Republics shall commence in the Gulf of Mexico, three leagues from land, opposite the mouth of the Rio Grande, otherwise called Rio Bravo del Norte, or opposite the mouth of '''it’s''' deepest branch, if it should have more than one branch emptying directly into the sea; from thence up the middle of that river, following the deepest channel, where it has more than one, to the point where it strikes the southern boundary of New Mexico; thence, westwardly, along the whole southern boundary of New Mexico (which runs north of the town called Paso) to '''it’s''' western termination; thence, northward, along the western line of New Mexico, until it intersects the first branch of the river Gila; (or if it should not intersect any branch of that river, then, to the point on the said line nearest to such branch, and thence in a direct line to the same;) thence down the middle of the said branch and of the said river, until it empties into the Rio Colorado; thence, across the Rio Colorado, following the division line between Upper and Lower California, to the Pacific Ocean.}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Tom Sawyer|page=219|passage=The mouth of the cave was up the hillside—an opening shaped like a letter A. '''It’s''' massive oaken door stood unbarred.}}
# {{misspelling of|en|its}}.
#* {{quote-video game|en|year=2014|title=Five Nights at Freddy's 2|author=Scott Cawthon|scene=night 6 ending|text=After being open only a few short weeks, Freddy Fazbear's Pizza is closing '''it{{'}}s''' doors.}}

